name: "looking_glass"
type: "tool"
about: "
## Looking Glass

Looking Glass is a web-based inspection and editing server for AiR-Poet units and
any folder-based software system. It is implemented as an .air program wrapping a
Python HTTP server.

### Invocation

    air looking_glass.air <root_folder>

The interpreter is called with `looking_glass.air` and a starting root folder.
Looking Glass scans that folder recursively, discovers all units and files, and
serves a live web UI at localhost on a configurable port.

### Core Principle

A better point of view is worth many smart points.

The system is designed to help humans and LLMs inspect a project at a level that
is simultaneously high (you can see the whole shape) and deterministic (nothing is
hidden, summarized away, or ambiguous). This is the 'looking glass' metaphor:
a lens that does not distort — it only makes things clearer.

This is especially valuable for observing an evolving code object production
process. Software is not a snapshot; it is a sequence of intentional changes.
Looking Glass makes the sequence visible alongside the current state.

---

## Views

### Root View
The landing page gives a single large editable textarea showing the selected
file. This is intentionally minimal: one thing in focus at a time.
- Syntax highlighting for .air, .py, .meta, .md and common formats
- Inline linting with inline error markers (no separate panel)
- Line numbers, matching bracket highlight
- Ctrl+S / Cmd+S saves

### Unit Panel (left sidebar)
- Tree of all discovered units and their files
- Click to open a file in the root view
- Icon indicators for file type (meta / implementation / test / plan / doc)
- Unit health indicator (tests passing, linting clean)

### Side-by-Side View
- Any two files can be opened in a split view for comparison
- Diff mode: highlight additions, deletions, modifications between the two panes
- The split can be vertical (two files side by side) or horizontal (stacked)
- Useful for comparing .air spec vs .py implementation, or two versions of a unit

### History Lane
- A collapsible right panel showing a timeline of snapshots for the open file
- Snapshots are saved automatically on each Save, or manually labeled
- Click a snapshot to view it in a read-only third pane (3-way: current / snapshot / diff)
- This makes the *evolving production process* visible: you can watch a unit grow
- Snapshots are stored as lightweight diffs in a .looking_glass/ folder at the root

### AI Export View
- A dedicated page that renders the project as llms.txt, context.md, and index.json
- These are the same machine-readable summaries used by LLM tooling
- The view lets you preview exactly what an LLM would see when reading the project
- Configurable: choose which units and files to include, set depth limits

---

## Navigation & Search

- **Zoom levels**: system → unit → file → line
  Each zoom level has its own default view. At system level you see the unit graph.
  At unit level you see the file list + meta. At file level you see the editor.
  At line level you can anchor a permalink to a specific line.
- **Full-text search**: Ctrl+K opens a quick-search bar across all files
  Results show file path, line number, and surrounding context
  Filtering by file type, unit, or regex is supported
- **Symbol jump**: Ctrl+P opens a file switcher (fuzzy match on filename or unit name)

---

## Annotation Layer

Annotations are notes attached to a unit, file, or specific line *without modifying
the source file*. They are stored in .looking_glass/annotations.json.
- An observer (human reviewer or LLM) can leave notes while in read-only mode
- Annotations are shown as margin icons; hovering reveals the note text
- Useful for design reviews, LLM analysis passes, and audit trails
- Annotations can be exported as a structured report

---

## Themes

Themes are applied globally via a selector in the top bar.
All themes are CSS variable sets; the editor (CodeMirror) and the UI both switch.

Standard themes:
  - dark          — default, low-eye-strain dark (Catppuccin Mocha palette)
  - light         — clean white, print-friendly
  - solarized     — Solarized Dark / Light toggle
  - high_contrast — WCAG AAA compliant, maximum legibility
  - deuteranopia  — color-blind friendly (no red/green distinction)

Custom themes can be added as .css files in .looking_glass/themes/.

---

## Read-Only Inspection Mode

Started with:

    air looking_glass.air <root_folder> --read-only

In this mode:
- All save buttons are hidden
- Textarea is rendered non-editable (but still selectable and copyable)
- Annotations are still allowed
- Intended for LLM inspection passes or code review sessions where accidental
  edits should be impossible

---

## Live Reload

Looking Glass watches the root folder with a filesystem watcher.
When a file changes (by an external editor, a test run, or an LLM tool):
- The open file in the UI refreshes automatically if it has no unsaved changes
- A small toast notification announces the change
- History Lane records the external change as a snapshot labeled 'external'

---

## File Discovery

On startup, Looking Glass scans the root folder:
1. Finds all .meta files — these define units
2. For each unit, collects sibling files matching the unit name pattern
3. All other files are listed under a virtual 'loose files' unit
4. Hidden folders (starting with .) and __pycache__ are excluded by default

The scan result is the project graph: nodes are units, edges are dependencies
declared in .meta files. This graph is visualized in the system-level zoom view
as an interactive node diagram.

---

## Implementation Notes

The server is a pure Python HTTP server (no framework dependency).
The .air file defines the routing table and handler dispatch as AiR tables.
The Python side implements file I/O, directory watching, snapshot storage,
and serving static assets.

The UI is vanilla JS + CodeMirror for the editor. No build step required.
All assets are inlined or served directly from the looking_glass/ unit folder.
"

components: {
    looking_glass_server
    looking_glass_ui
    looking_glass_snapshot
    looking_glass_search
    looking_glass_theme
}
